;; ============================================================================

;; CONTROL FLOW  —  port of Simple's RegionNode, IfNode, LoopNode, CProjNode,
;;                  StartNode, StopNode, ReturnNode, XCtrlNode, NeverNode
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; These are one file because their peepholes are one system: a Region with a dead path drops it,
;; an If on a constant kills one projection, that projection turns to XCTRL, and the Region that
;; merged it drops a path in turn. Dead control propagates through all of them, and splitting the
;; halves of that cascade across ten files puts one rewrite in ten places.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; REGION — the control merge, and the most peephole-dense node in the graph
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Inputs 1..n are the incoming control paths. Input 0 is unused (it is where a data node keeps its
;; control, and a Region has none).
;;
;; ## compute
;;
;;     if in-progress: CONTROL          (see the construction window below)
;;     else:           XCONTROL meet over all inputs
;;
;; Seeding with XCONTROL and meeting up is the whole of reachability: a Region is reachable exactly
;; when SOME path reaching it is. Note the direction — this is the optimistic seed, and a Region
;; nobody has analysed yet reports "unreachable" until evidence arrives.
;;
;; > THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE PROVEN-TYPE LAW IN CONCRETE FORM: seeding with XCONTROL means a merge
;; > that nobody has analysed reports PROVABLY UNREACHABLE out of nothing. `~ctrl` is merely the
;; > high element of the control axis, not a claim anyone computed. A rewrite that acts on it is
;; > acting on the absence of information. Any irreversible rewrite must ask `ty-proven?` first.
;;
;; ## idealize — Simple's rules, in the order it applies them
;;
;; 1. IN-PROGRESS: do nothing at all. Bail before every other rule.
;;
;; 2. DEAD PATH. Find an input whose type is XCONTROL and remove it — and with it the matching
;;    value from EVERY Phi on this Region, in the same operation.
;;
;;    Simple's `removeDeadPath` cannot use the obvious output iterator: deleting a Phi input can
;;    recursively delete the Phi ITSELF, which shuffles the output array and makes the loop skip an
;;    unrelated Phi. So it repeats the scan until the output count stops changing. Port that loop
;;    faithfully; the naive version drops a Phi arm on rare occasions, which is a miscompile that
;;    typechecks.
;;
;;    Exception: never delete the ENTRY path of a Loop. Removing the back edge and leaving a
;;    single-entry Region is fine and folds the Loop away; removing the entry instead promotes the
;;    back edge to entry and corrupts the loop structure.
;;
;; 3. SINGLE INPUT: `nIns()==2 && !hasPhi()` -> become that input. Guarded on having no Phis
;;    because a one-input Phi collapses on its own, and doing both at once races.
;;
;;    >>> THIS RULE IS WHAT MAKES INLINING WORK. A Fun IS a Region and a Parm IS a Phi, so
;;    >>> "inlining" is: bypass the Call, leave the Fun with one input, and let THIS rule collapse
;;    >>> it while the Parms collapse into the caller's actual arguments. Nothing is cloned. See
;;    >>> src/node/call.coil. If this rule is weakened, inlining silently stops happening.
;;
;; 4. EMPTY DIAMOND: `if (pred) {} else {}` — a Region with no Phi users whose two inputs are both
;;    CProjs of the SAME If. Replace with that If's control directly. This is what deletes a
;;    conditional whose arms did nothing.
;;
;; 5. FLATTEN STACKED REGIONS: a Region feeding a Region, neither a Loop, with no value stranded
;;    between them. Splice the inner one's paths into the outer and extend every Phi accordingly.
;;    `hasMidUser` is the guard: if anything between the two Regions uses a value from the inner one
;;    other than a Phi on the outer, Simple declines — "difficult to correctly fuse without
;;    extensive testing, so just ignore".
;;
;; ## The in-progress window  (LAW, docs/LAYOUT.md §8)
;;
;; A Region is IN PROGRESS while its last input is null. While in progress it reports CONTROL
;; unconditionally, its Phis report their DECLARED types, and it is never GVN-equal to anything
;; (`eq` returns `!inProgress()`).
;;
;; A loop body must be built AND PEEPHOLED inside that window. Final Simple's `endLoop` then closes
;; control and immediately fills every Phi backedge as one protected operation. A Phi's independent
;; null-final-input window prevents folding during that interval; no peephole belongs between the
;; two mutations.
;;
;; ## Region and Phi arity are ONE invariant
;;
;; Dropping a path drops the matching value from every Phi on that Region, in the same operation.
;; There is deliberately no `region-del-path!` that does not also touch the Phis. Diverging for even
;; one peephole gives a Phi reading the wrong arm — a miscompile that typechecks cleanly.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; IF — the two-way branch
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; Inputs: 0 = control, 1 = predicate. It is a MULTI node: it produces a tuple, and the two CProjs
;; select from it. `uctrl` is deliberately null — there is no single successor.
;;
;; ## compute  — returns one of four TypeTuples
;;
;;     ctrl not reachable        -> IF_NEITHER
;;     predicate type is HIGH    -> IF_NEITHER   (wait for it to fall; do not guess a side)
;;     predicate is constant     -> IF_FALSE if zero/null/empty-fidx, else IF_TRUE
;;     adding zero changes it    -> IF_TRUE      (the predicate provably cannot be zero/null)
;;     otherwise                 -> IF_BOTH
;;
;; The fourth case is the one worth noticing: `!t.makeZero().isa(t)` asks "would admitting null
;; widen this type?" If yes, null was not in it, so the test cannot fail. That is how a null check
;; on an already-non-null reference deletes itself, and it is the mechanism a discharged TypeScript
;; annotation rides on.
;;
;; ## idealize — dominating identical test
;;
;; Walk UP the immediate-dominator tree. If an enclosing If tests the same predicate, then on this
;; branch the answer is already known: replace our predicate with the constant 1 or 0 depending on
;; which CProj we came down through. This is how a repeated bounds check or repeated null check
;; folds away, and it costs one dominator walk rather than a dataflow pass.
;;
;; ## The multi window  (LAW, docs/LAYOUT.md §8)
;;
;; An If is IN PROGRESS until ALL of its projections exist. Peephole them one at a time and the
;; first folds to ~ctrl, the If loses its last use, the kill cascade takes it, and the sibling then
;; rewrites against a corpse whose inputs were cleared but whose type was not — a stale buffer word.
;; Flat, that word happened to be pinned Start and the answer was accidentally right; one level of
;; nesting down it was a dead node.
;;
;; `if-arms!` is therefore the ONLY sanctioned constructor: it opens the window, builds both CProjs,
;; and closes it. There is deliberately no way to make one arm alone.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; LOOP, START, STOP, RETURN, CPROJ, XCTRL, NEVER
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; LOOP is a Region whose input 1 is the entry and input 2 is the BACK EDGE. Everything Region does
;; applies; the differences are that the entry path is never deleted, and that a Phi on a Loop gets
;; the widening treatment described in src/node/phi.coil.
;;
;; START is the root and a MULTI: it projects the initial control, the initial memory, and the
;; arguments. Constants are pinned to it, which is why every use of the same constant GVNs to one
;; node in the start block.
;;
;; STOP is the sink. Every Return is an input. The program is exactly what Stop keeps alive — which
;; is why a dropped Return is invisible to the verifier: a Stop with fewer inputs is a structurally
;; valid Stop.
;;
;; RETURN carries control, memory, the value, and RPC. Inlining nulls the RPC edge; the enclosing
;; Fun remains separate node metadata.
;;
;; CPROJ selects one control edge out of a multi. It is a block head.
;;
;; XCTRL is the unreachable-control constant — the value a folded-away branch becomes.
;;
;; NEVER is control that cannot be PROVEN dead but is known not to return, e.g. an infinite loop
;; that the loop-tree pass broke. GCM's `better` treats it as maximally good placement, so nothing
;; is ever hoisted past it.
;;
;; ============================================================================
;; STATUS: implemented. Every rule above is live and tested. What is NOT here, and why:
;;   - Fun/Parm (a Region and a Phi) and Call/CallEnd  -> the calls unit
;;   - Loop `forceExit` for infinite loops             -> needs the loop tree
;;   - Start's memory and argument projections          -> need memory types
;; Each is named where it would go, and reaching one is a hard error, never a quiet default.
;; ============================================================================
(module aot.node.control)
(import "coil.primitive" :as primitive)
(import "coil.alloc" :as alloc :use [unwrap-ptr create box!])
(import "coil.arraylist" :use *)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)
(import "aot.node.cfg" :use *)
(import "aot.node.constant" :use *)
(import "aot.type.type" :use *)

;; If projection indices. Simple: index 0 is the TRUE arm, index 1 is the FALSE arm.
(const IF-TRUE-IDX 0)

(const IF-FALSE-IDX 1)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; START
;;
;; The external world calling in. Simple's Start is a LoopNode (its input 1 is the Stop, closing
;; what Simple calls the Grand Cycle between the code we know and the code we do not), and a Multi:
;; it projects the initial control, the initial memory and the arguments.
;;
;; Here it is a Multi with no dominator and idepth 0. The memory and argument members arrive with
;; the memory unit; today it projects control alone.
(defstruct StartNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (arg-ty i64)])

(impl NodeOps StartNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Start")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; Start is the root of the program. Folding it to a constant would delete the world.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr StartNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> i64) (ty-tuple-of2 TY-CTRL (.arg-ty self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr StartNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-START))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> i64) 101))

(impl CFGOps StartNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> bool) true)
  ;; No immediate dominator, and idepth 0. Every dominator walk terminates here.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr StartNode))] (-> i64) 0))

(defn start-make [(arg-ty i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [StartNode] (alloc/create [StartNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.arg-ty p) arg-ty)
    (n-init! d OP-START)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; STOP
;;
;; The sink. Every Return is an input, and the program is exactly what Stop keeps alive — which is
;; why a dropped Return is invisible to the verifier: a Stop with fewer inputs is a structurally
;; valid Stop. Only running the program notices.
;;
;; `idealize` drops inputs that have gone dead (Simple tests `in(i).nIns()==0`), and reports progress
;; only if the count actually changed.
(defstruct StopNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

(impl NodeOps StopNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Stop")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr StopNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> i64) TY-CTRL)
  (idealize [(self (ptr StopNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (stop-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr StopNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-STOP))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> i64) 103))

(impl CFGOps StopNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; Final Simple treats Stop as the synthetic head of the sink block. It can have several Return
  ;; predecessors, so no single immediate dominator exists.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr StopNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr StopNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)
                    cnt (n-nins d)
                    (mut i) 0
                    (mut depth) 0]
                (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                          (break 0)
                          (do
                            (match (n-in d (load i))
                              (Some [ret]
                                    (match (cfg-of ret)
                                      (Some [_]
                                            (let [candidate (+ (cfg-idepth-of ret) 1)]
                                              (if (> candidate (load depth))
                                                  (set! (mut depth) candidate)
                                                  0)))
                                      (None [] 0)))
                              (None [] 0))
                            (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                            (continue))))
                (load depth))))

;; Drop every input that has gone dead. Reports progress only when the count changed.
(defn stop-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [before (n-nins d)
        (mut i) 0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins d))
              (break 0)
              (match (n-in d (load i))
                (Some [v]
                      (if (n-dead? v)
                          (do (n-del-def! d (load i)) (continue))
                          (do (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1)) (continue))))
                (None [] (do (n-del-def! d (load i)) (continue))))))
    (if (!= before (n-nins d)) (Some d) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))

(defn stop-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [StopNode] (alloc/create [StopNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-STOP)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

(defn stop-add-return! [(stop (dyn NodeOps)) (ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (n-add-def! stop (Some ret)) (invalidate-cfg!) 0))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; XCTRL — the unreachable-control constant
;;
;; What a folded-away branch becomes. `is-const?` is true so the engine never tries to replace it
;; with a Constant, which would loop.
(defstruct XCtrlNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

(impl NodeOps XCtrlNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "XCtrl")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr XCtrlNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) TY-XCTRL)
  (idealize [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr XCtrlNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-XCTRL))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) 107))

(impl CFGOps XCtrlNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (n-in (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr XCtrlNode))] (-> i64) 0))

(defn xctrl-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [XCtrlNode] (alloc/create [XCtrlNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-XCTRL)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (n-set-ty! d TY-XCTRL)
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; REGION — the control merge, and the most peephole-dense node in the graph
;;
;; Inputs 1..n are the incoming control paths. Input 0 is unused (it is where a data node keeps its
;; control, and a Region has none).
(defstruct RegionNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

;; A Region is IN PROGRESS while its LAST input is null.
;;
;; While in progress it reports CONTROL unconditionally, its Phis report their DECLARED types, and
;; it is never GVN-equal to anything. A loop body must be built AND PEEPHOLED inside that window;
;; loop finalization then closes control and immediately fills every Phi backedge.
(defn region-in-progress? [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (if (<= (n-nins r) 1)
      false
      (match (n-in r (- (n-nins r) 1)) (Some [_] false) (None [] true))))

(defn region-has-phi? [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [cnt (n-nouts r)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut yes) false]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (load yes))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out r (load i))
                  (Some [u]
                        ;; Parm is the Phi family attached to Fun. Omitting it lets a folding Fun
                        ;; collapse first and rewires the still-live Parm's region edge to the
                        ;; caller, after which it can no longer perform argument substitution.
                        (if (or (= (n-op u) OP-PHI) (= (n-op u) OP-MEMPHI) (= (n-op u) OP-PARM))
                            (set! (mut yes) true)
                            0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load yes)))

;; Seed with XCONTROL and meet up: a Region is reachable exactly when SOME path reaching it is.
;;
;; >>> THE PROVEN-TYPE TRAP IN CONCRETE FORM <<<
;; Seeding with XCONTROL means a merge NOBODY HAS ANALYSED reports "provably unreachable" out of
;; nothing. `~ctrl` is merely the high element of the control axis, not a claim anyone computed. Any
;; IRREVERSIBLE rewrite acting on it must first prove the type.
(defn region-compute [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (region-in-progress? r)
      TY-CTRL
      (let [cnt (n-nins r)
            (mut i) 1
            (mut t) TY-XCTRL]
        (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                  (break 0)
                  (do
                    (match (n-in r (load i))
                      (Some [v] (set! (mut t) (ty-meet (load t) (n-ty v))))
                      (None [] 0))
                    (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                    (continue))))
        (load t))))

;; Find an input whose type is XCONTROL, starting at `from`. 0 means every input is live.
(defn region--find-dead-input [(r (dyn NodeOps)) (from i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [cnt (n-nins r)
        (mut i) from
        (mut found) 0]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (!= (load found) 0))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in r (load i))
                  (Some [v] (if (= (n-ty v) TY-XCTRL) (set! (mut found) (load i)) 0))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; Drop path `i` from `r` AND the matching value from every Phi on `r`, in ONE operation.
;;
;; Region arity and Phi arity are a single invariant. Diverging for even one peephole gives a Phi
;; reading the wrong arm — a miscompile that typechecks cleanly. That is why there is deliberately
;; no path-removal entry point that does not also touch the Phis.
;;
;; The repeat-until-stable scan is Simple's, and the reason is subtle: deleting a Phi input can
;; recursively delete the PHI ITSELF, which shuffles the output array and makes a single pass skip
;; an unrelated Phi. Simple: "on rare occasions we repeat the loop to get all the Phis."
(defn region-del-path! [(r (dyn NodeOps)) (path i64)] (-> i64)
  (do
    (let [(mut prev) -1]
      (loop (if (= (load prev) (n-nouts r))
                (break 0)
                (do
                  (set! (mut prev) (n-nouts r))
                  (let [(mut i) 0]
                    (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nouts r))
                              (break 0)
                              (do
                                (match (n-out r (load i))
                                  (Some [u]
                                        (if (and (phi-like? (n-op u))
                                                 (= (n-nins u) (n-nins r)))
                                            (do (n-del-def! u path) 0)
                                            0))
                                  (None [] 0))
                                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                                (continue)))))
                  (continue)))))
    (n-del-def! r path)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    0))

;; Simple's `deadPath`. `skip-start` skips input 1, which a Loop needs: removing a Loop's ENTRY path
;; would promote the back edge to entry and corrupt the loop structure. Removing the BACK edge is
;; fine — it leaves a single-entry Region, which then folds away.
(defn region-dead-path! [(r (dyn NodeOps)) (skip-entry bool)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [path (region--find-dead-input r (if skip-entry 2 1))]
    (if (= path 0)
        (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
        (do
          (region-del-path! r path)
          ;; Slot zero is the reserved null control input. Reaching one input means every real path
          ;; was dead; replace the Region immediately instead of leaving a zero-path Region whose
          ;; next idealization would read input 1.
          (if (= (n-nins r) 1) (Some (xctrl-make)) (Some r))))))

(defn region-idealize [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  ;; 1. IN PROGRESS: bail before every other rule.
  (if (region-in-progress? r)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      ;; 2. DEAD PATH.
      (match (region-dead-path! r false)
        (Some [x] (Some x))
        (None []
              ;; 3. SINGLE INPUT: become that input.
              ;;
              ;; >>> THIS RULE IS WHAT MAKES INLINING WORK. A Fun IS a Region and a Parm IS a Phi,
              ;; >>> so inlining is: bypass the Call, leave the Fun with one input, and let THIS rule
              ;; >>> collapse it while the Parms collapse into the caller's arguments. Nothing is
              ;; >>> cloned. Weaken this and inlining silently stops happening.
              ;;
              ;; Guarded on having no Phis: a one-input Phi collapses on its own, and doing both at
              ;; once races.
              (if (and (= (n-nins r) 2) (not (region-has-phi? r)))
                  (n-in r 1)
                  ;; 4. EMPTY DIAMOND: `if (pred) {} else {}` — no Phi users, and paths 1 and 2 are
                  ;; CProjs of the SAME If. Both arms reach here, so the If contributes exactly its
                  ;; own control, once.
                  ;;
                  ;; >>> A DIVERGENCE FROM SIMPLE, DELIBERATE AND RECORDED. <<<
                  ;;
                  ;; Simple guards this rule with `nIns()>3` — three or more paths — and then opens
                  ;; with `if (nIns()==3) return iff.ctrl();`, which under that guard is UNREACHABLE.
                  ;; So Simple's live path only ever handles the FAT case, and the two-path diamond
                  ;; its own code describes never fires. That reads as an off-by-one (`>3` for
                  ;; `>=3`) rather than intent.
                  ;;
                  ;; Both cases are individually correct, so we implement BOTH:
                  ;;   two paths  -> the Region IS the If's control; become it
                  ;;   fat        -> rewrite path 1 to the If's control and delete path 2
                  (if (and (not (region-has-phi? r)) (region--empty-diamond? r))
                      (if (= (n-nins r) 3)
                          ;; CProj -> If -> the If's own control. Simple: `p1.in(0)` is the If,
                          ;; and `iff.ctrl()` is ITS input 0.
                          (Some (n-in! (n-in! (n-in! r 1) 0) 0))
                          (region--collapse-fat-diamond! r))
                      ;; 5. FLATTEN STACKED REGIONS. Loops and Funs are excluded by requiring the
                      ;; exact Region opcode on both sides. `region--has-mid-user?` is the safety
                      ;; guard: a value consumed between the inner and outer merges cannot be
                      ;; distributed correctly by this local rewrite.
                      (region--flatten-stacked! r)))))))

;; Is `p` a Phi-like node whose controlling Region is `r`?
(defn region--phi-on? [(p (dyn NodeOps)) (r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (and (phi-like? (n-op p))
       (match (n-in p 0) (Some [pr] (n-same? pr r)) (None [] false))))

;; Simple's `hasMidUser`. Every output of the inner Region must be either the outer Region itself,
;; or a Phi whose every data user is another Phi on the outer Region. Anything else consumes a
;; value in-between the merges. Register dependencies on the blockers so the outer Region is
;; retried if later graph rewrites remove them.
(defn region--has-mid-user? [(outer (dyn NodeOps)) (inner (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (let [cnt (n-nouts inner)
        (mut i) 0
        (mut found) false]
    (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (load found))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-out inner (load i))
                  (None [] 0)
                  (Some [use]
                        (if (n-same? use outer)
                            0
                            (if (phi-like? (n-op use))
                                (let [dcnt (n-nouts use)
                                      (mut j) 0]
                                  (loop (if (or (>= (load j) dcnt) (load found))
                                            (break 0)
                                            (do
                                              (match (n-out use (load j))
                                                (None [] 0)
                                                (Some [data]
                                                      (if (region--phi-on? data outer)
                                                          0
                                                          (do
                                                            (n-add-dep! use outer)
                                                            (n-add-dep! data outer)
                                                            (set! (mut found) true)
                                                            0))))
                                              (set! (mut j) (+ (load j) 1))
                                              (continue)))))
                                (do
                                  (n-add-dep! use outer)
                                  (set! (mut found) true)
                                  0)))))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load found)))

;; Flatten the first eligible Region input, matching Simple's rule order and doing one rewrite per
;; visit. Outer Phis either splice the matching inner Phi's values or duplicate the outer value
;; across every inner path. The old outer slot becomes XCtrl; the ordinary dead-path rule removes
;; it and the matching old Phi value on the next visit.
(defn region--flatten-stacked! [(outer (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (or (!= (n-op outer) OP-REGION) (<= (n-nins outer) 2))
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      (let [cnt (n-nins outer)
            (mut i) 1
            (mut inner) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
        (loop (if (or (>= (load i) cnt) (match (load inner) (Some [_] true) (None [] false)))
                  (break 0)
                  (do
                    (match (n-in outer (load i))
                      (Some [candidate]
                            (if (and (= (n-op candidate) OP-REGION)
                                     (and (> (n-nins candidate) 2)
                                          (not (region--has-mid-user? outer candidate))))
                                (set! (mut inner) (Some candidate))
                                0))
                      (None [] 0))
                    (if (match (load inner) (Some [_] true) (None [] false))
                        0
                        (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1)))
                    (continue))))
        (match (load inner)
          (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
          (Some [r]
                (do
                  (let [outcnt (n-nouts outer)
                        (mut oi) 0]
                    (loop (if (>= (load oi) outcnt)
                              (break 0)
                              (do
                                (match (n-out outer (load oi))
                                  (Some [phi]
                                        (if (phi-like? (n-op phi))
                                            (let [old (n-in! phi (load i))
                                                  splice (region--phi-on? old r)
                                                  (mut j) 1]
                                              (loop (if (>= (load j) (n-nins r))
                                                        (break 0)
                                                        (do
                                                          (n-add-def! phi
                                                                      (Some (if splice
                                                                                (n-in! old (load j))
                                                                                old)))
                                                          (set! (mut j) (+ (load j) 1))
                                                          (continue))))
                                              (code-add! phi))
                                            0))
                                  (None [] 0))
                                (set! (mut oi) (+ (load oi) 1))
                                (continue)))))
                  (let [(mut j) 1]
                    (loop (if (>= (load j) (n-nins r))
                              (break 0)
                              (do
                                (region-add-path! outer (n-in! r (load j)))
                                (set! (mut j) (+ (load j) 1))
                                (continue)))))
                  (n-set-def! outer (load i) (Some (xctrl-make)))
                  (Some outer)))))))

;; The fat case: paths 1 and 2 are both arms of one If, and there are other paths besides. Replace
;; path 1 with the If's own control and drop path 2 — the If reaches here exactly once.
;;
;; `region-del-path!` rather than a raw delete, because a Phi on this Region must lose the same arm
;; in the same operation. There are no Phis here by the guard above, but routing through the one
;; entry point is what keeps that invariant true by construction rather than by argument.
(defn region--collapse-fat-diamond! [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [iff (n-in! (n-in! r 1) 0)]
    (n-set-def! r 1 (n-in iff 0))
    (region-del-path! r 2)
    (Some r)))

;; Are inputs 1 and 2 both CProjs of the same If?
(defn region--empty-diamond? [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (match (n-in r 1)
    (None [] false)
    (Some [p1]
          (match (n-in r 2)
            (None [] false)
            (Some [p2]
                  (if (and (= (n-op p1) OP-CPROJ) (= (n-op p2) OP-CPROJ))
                      (match (n-in p1 0)
                        (None [] false)
                        (Some [m1]
                              (match (n-in p2 0)
                                (None [] false)
                                (Some [m2] (and (n-same? m1 m2) (= (n-op m1) OP-IF))))))
                      false))))))

;; Region's idepth is the MAX over its inputs, not one-plus-parent: a merge is as deep as its
;; deepest predecessor.
(defn region-idepth [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (n-idepth-valid? r)
      (n-idepth-cached r)
      (let [cnt (n-nins r)
            (mut i) 0
            (mut d) 0]
        ;; Seed the cache BEFORE recursing, so a cycle through the back edge terminates instead of
        ;; recursing forever. Simple gets this from its own lazy-cache discipline.
        (n-set-idepth! r 0)
        (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
                  (break 0)
                  (do
                    (match (n-in r (load i))
                      (Some [v]
                            (let [vd (cfg-idepth-of v)]
                              (if (> vd (load d)) (set! (mut d) vd) 0)))
                      (None [] 0))
                    (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                    (continue))))
        (n-set-idepth! r (+ (load d) 1)))))

(impl NodeOps RegionNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Region")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; While in progress a Region reports CONTROL, which is not high-or-constant, so no guard is
  ;; needed for that. But a Region that folds to XCTRL must become the XCtrl node through
  ;; `idealize`, not through constant replacement, so refuse the fold here.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr RegionNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> i64) (region-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr RegionNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (region-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  ;; NEVER equal while in progress: two half-built merges are not interchangeable.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr RegionNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-REGION)
                false
                (not (region-in-progress? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> i64) 109))

(impl CFGOps RegionNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr RegionNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (region-idom (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr RegionNode))] (-> i64) (region-idepth (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

;; A Region's immediate dominator is the LCA of all its LIVE inputs. High inputs are skipped: a path
;; nobody has proven reachable does not constrain dominance.
(defn region-idom [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [cnt (n-nins r)
        (mut i) 1
        (mut lca) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) cnt)
              (break 0)
              (do
                (match (n-in r (load i))
                  (Some [v]
                        (if (ty-high? (n-ty v))
                            0
                            (set! (mut lca) (idom-lca-nodes v (load lca)))))
                  (None [] 0))
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))
    (load lca)))

(defn region-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [RegionNode] (alloc/create [RegionNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-REGION)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; slot 0 unused
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

(defn region-add-path! [(r (dyn NodeOps)) (path (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (n-add-def! r (Some path)) (invalidate-cfg!) 0))

;; Open the in-progress window: append a null final input.
(defn region-open! [(r (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (n-add-def! r (None [(dyn NodeOps)])) (invalidate-cfg!) 0))

;; Close it by filling that null with the real final path.
(defn region-close! [(r (dyn NodeOps)) (path (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (not (region-in-progress? r))
      (broken "region-close!: the region is not in progress")
      (do (n-set-def! r (- (n-nins r) 1) (Some path)) (invalidate-cfg!) 0)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The generic dominator walks
;;
;; These live here rather than in `aot.node.cfg` because they need to turn a `(dyn NodeOps)` back
;; into a control view, and `cfg-of` — the one place that knows which opcodes are control — needs
;; the concrete structs, which are in this module.

;; The one place that knows which opcodes are control. Simple's `instanceof CFGNode`.
;;
;; Do not replicate this test anywhere else. A new control opcode that only some sites learn about
;; would be treated as a data node, and GCM would try to place it.
(defn cfg-of [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn CFGOps)))
  (let [op (n-op n)]
    (if (= op OP-START)
        (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr StartNode) (hdr n))))
        (if (= op OP-STOP)
            (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr StopNode) (hdr n))))
            (if (= op OP-XCTRL)
                (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr XCtrlNode) (hdr n))))
                (if (= op OP-REGION)
                    (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr RegionNode) (hdr n))))
                    (cfg-of-more n)))))))

(defn cfg? [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (match (cfg-of n) (Some [_] true) (None [] false)))

;; The immediate dominator of any control node, or None. A data node has no dominator and asking is
;; a hard error rather than a quiet None — the caller has confused a value for control.
(defn cfg-idom-of [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (cfg-of n)
    (Some [c] (cfg-idom c))
    (None [] (do (panic-i "cfg-idom-of: not a control node (op)" (n-op n)) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))))

(defn cfg-idepth-of [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (cfg-of n)
    (Some [c] (cfg-idepth c))
    (None [] (do (panic-i "cfg-idepth-of: not a control node (op)" (n-op n)) 0))))

(defn cfg-block-head-of? [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (match (cfg-of n) (Some [c] (block-head? c)) (None [] false)))

;; The default idepth for a kind with a single control predecessor: one plus the parent's.
(defn cfg-idepth-default [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (n-idepth-valid? n)
      (n-idepth-cached n)
      (do
        (n-set-idepth! n 0)  ; seed before recursing, so a cycle terminates
        (n-set-idepth! n
                       (+ 1
                          (match (cfg-idom-of n)
                            (Some [p] (cfg-idepth-of p))
                            (None [] 0)))))))

;; Walk two dominator chains upward in parallel, stepping whichever side is deeper, until they meet.
;; `None` on either side means no common dominator.
;;
;; Simple notes this does NOT cache and can be linear in the program size — accepted deliberately,
;; because a cached dominator in a graph peepholes are rewriting goes stale, and a stale dominator
;; schedules a node outside its own dominance region.
(defn idom-lca-nodes [(a (dyn NodeOps)) (b (Option (dyn NodeOps)))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match b
    (None [] (Some a))
    (Some [b0]
          (let [(mut lhs) (Some a)
                (mut rhs) (Some b0)
                (mut out) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                (mut guard) 0]
            (loop
              ;; A safety net, not part of the algorithm: a malformed chain would otherwise spin
              ;; forever, and a hang is a far worse failure than a named panic.
              (if (> (load guard) 1000000)
                  (do (broken "idom-lca-nodes: dominator walk did not converge") (break 0))
                  (match (load lhs)
                    (None [] (break 0))
                    (Some [l]
                          (match (load rhs)
                            (None [] (break 0))
                            (Some [r]
                                  (if (n-same? l r)
                                      (do (set! (mut out) (Some l)) (break 0))
                                      (let [ld (cfg-idepth-of l)
                                            rd (cfg-idepth-of r)]
                                        (do
                                          (if (>= ld rd) (set! (mut lhs) (cfg-idom-of l)) 0)
                                          (if (<= ld rd) (set! (mut rhs) (cfg-idom-of r)) 0)
                                          (set! (mut guard) (+ (load guard) 1))
                                          (continue))))))))))
            (load out)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; LOOP — a Region whose input 1 is the entry and input 2 is the BACK EDGE
;;
;; Everything Region does applies. The differences:
;;   - `compute` reports the ENTRY's type, not the meet: a loop is reachable exactly when its entry
;;     is. The back edge cannot make a loop reachable that nothing enters.
;;   - the entry path is never deleted by dead-path removal (removing it would promote the back edge
;;     to entry and corrupt the loop structure); the BACK edge may be, leaving a single-entry Region
;;     that then folds away.
;;   - `idom` is the ENTRY directly, bypassing Region's LCA: the back edge dominates nothing.
(defstruct LoopNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (pad i64)])

(defn loop-entry [(l (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! l 1))

(defn loop-back [(l (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! l 2))

(defn loop-compute [(l (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (region-in-progress? l)
      TY-CTRL
      (match (n-in l 1) (Some [e] (n-ty e)) (None [] TY-CTRL))))

(defn loop-idealize [(l (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (region-in-progress? l)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      ;; Dead-path removal, skipping the ENTRY.
      (match (region-dead-path! l true)
        (Some [x] (Some x))
        (None []
              ;; A Loop that lost its back edge is an ordinary single-entry Region.
              (if (and (= (n-nins l) 2) (not (region-has-phi? l)))
                  (n-in l 1)
                  (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))

(impl NodeOps LoopNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Loop")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr LoopNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> i64) (loop-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr LoopNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (loop-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr LoopNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-LOOP)
                false
                (not (region-in-progress? (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> i64) 113))

(impl CFGOps LoopNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  (block-head? [(self (ptr LoopNode))] (-> bool) true)
  ;; The ENTRY, bypassing Region's LCA: a back edge dominates nothing.
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr LoopNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 1))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr LoopNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

;; Build a Loop over `entry`, OPEN: input 2 is null until `loop-close!` wires the back edge.
;;
;; THE PROTOCOL:
;;   1. `loop-make` the loop over its entry
;;   2. build the body INSIDE this window
;;   3. peephole the body — still inside the window
;;   4. final Simple's `endLoop` calls `loop-close!`
;;   5. it immediately closes every materialized Phi before any peephole can run
;;
;; `scope-end-loop!` owns steps 4 and 5. The low-level mutation remains public for graph tests, but
;; frontend lowering must not split finalization or expose its intermediate state.
(defn loop-make [(entry (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [LoopNode] (alloc/create [LoopNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.pad p) 0)
    (n-init! d OP-LOOP)
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; slot 0 unused
    (n-add-def! d (Some entry))  ; slot 1 entry
    (n-add-def! d (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))  ; slot 2 back edge — OPEN
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d TY-CTRL)
    d))

(defn loop-in-progress? [(l (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool)
  (region-in-progress? l))

(defn loop-close! [(l (dyn NodeOps)) (back (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (not (region-in-progress? l))
      (broken "loop-close!: the loop is not in progress — control was closed twice")
      (do (n-set-def! l 2 (Some back)) (invalidate-cfg!) 0)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; IF — the two-way branch
;;
;; Inputs: 0 = control, 1 = predicate. A MULTI: it produces a two-member tuple, and the two CProjs
;; select from it. Member 0 is TRUE, member 1 is FALSE.
(defstruct IfNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (never bool)])

(defn if-ctrl [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! n 0))

(defn if-pred [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (n-in n 1))

;; Simple's four outcomes.
;;
;;   ctrl not reachable      -> IF_NEITHER
;;   predicate HIGH          -> IF_NEITHER   (wait for it to fall; do NOT guess a side)
;;   predicate constant      -> IF_FALSE for JavaScript's falsy constants, else IF_TRUE
;;   adding zero widens it   -> IF_TRUE      (the predicate provably cannot be zero)
;;   otherwise               -> IF_BOTH
;;
;; The fourth case is the interesting one: "would admitting zero widen this type?" If yes, zero was
;; not in it, so the test cannot fail. That is how a null check on an already-non-null reference
;; deletes itself, and it is the mechanism a discharged TypeScript annotation rides on.
;;
;; IF_NEITHER from a high predicate is NOT "both arms are dead". It means nobody has decided yet.
(defn if-compute [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [ct (n-ty (if-ctrl d))]
    (if (and (!= ct TY-CTRL) (!= ct TY-BOT))
        (ty-if-neither)
        ;; A Never has no predicate: it is control that cannot be proven dead.
        (match (if-pred d)
          (None [] (ty-if-both))
          (Some [p]
                (let [t
                      ;; JavaScript source constants cross semantic boundaries boxed. Truthiness
                      ;; observes the payload value, not merely the singleton representation tag;
                      ;; otherwise Box(0) and Box(1) are both just `dyn:int` and neither branch can
                      ;; fold. This is the JS-specific extension of Simple's constant predicate
                      ;; rule, expressed structurally so Box remains the dynamic value.
                      (if (and (= (n-op p) OP-BOX) (> (n-nins p) 1))
                          (n-ty (n-in! p 1))
                          (n-ty p))]
                  (if (ty-high? t)
                      (ty-if-neither)
                      (if (ty-constant? t)
                          (if (ty-js-falsy-constant? t) (ty-if-false) (ty-if-true))
                          ;; Does admitting zero widen the type? If so, zero was not in it.
                          (if (and (ty-int? t) (not (ty-isa? (ty-int-con 0) t)))
                              (ty-if-true)
                              (ty-if-both))))))))))

;; Hunt UP the dominator tree for an enclosing If on the SAME predicate. On this branch the answer
;; is already known, so replace the predicate with the constant that arm implies.
;;
;; This is how a repeated bounds check or null check folds away — one dominator walk, not a dataflow
;; pass. `n-add-dep!` on each dominator visited, so a later change up the chain retries this.
(defn if--true-arm-cast-source [(candidate (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (!= (n-op candidate) OP-CAST)
      (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
      (match (n-in candidate 0)
        (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
        (Some [ctrl]
              (if (or (!= (n-op ctrl) OP-CPROJ) (!= (cproj-idx ctrl) IF-TRUE-IDX))
                  (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                  (match (n-in candidate 1)
                    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
                    (Some [source]
                          (let [iff (n-in! ctrl 0)]
                            (if (and (= (n-op iff) OP-IF)
                                     (match (if-pred iff)
                                       (Some [tested] (n-same? tested source))
                                       (None [] false)))
                                (Some source)
                                (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))))))))

(defn if-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (if-pred d)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [pred]
          (if (ty-high-or-const? (n-ty pred))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
              (let [(mut prior) (Some d)
                    (mut dom) (cfg-idom-of d)
                    (mut out) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
                    (mut guard) 0]
                (loop (if (> (load guard) 100000)
                          (do (broken "if-idealize: dominator walk did not converge") (break 0))
                          (match (load dom)
                            (None [] (break 0))
                            (Some [dm]
                                  (do
                                    (n-add-dep! dm d)
                                    (if (and (= (n-op dm) OP-IF)
                                             (match (load prior) (Some [pr] (= (n-op pr) OP-CPROJ)) (None [] false)))
                                        (match (if-pred dm)
                                          (None [] 0)
                                          (Some [dp]
                                                (if (or (n-same? dp pred)
                                                        (match (if--true-arm-cast-source pred)
                                                          (Some [source] (n-same? dp source))
                                                          (None [] false)))
                                                    (do
                                                      (n-set-def! d
                                                                  1
                                                                  (Some (con-int (if (= (match (load prior)
                                                                                          (Some [pr] (cproj-idx pr))
                                                                                          (None [] 0))
                                                                                        IF-TRUE-IDX)
                                                                                     1
                                                                                     0))))
                                                      (set! (mut out) (Some d))
                                                      (break 0))
                                                    0)))
                                        0)
                                    (set! (mut prior) (Some dm))
                                    (set! (mut dom) (cfg-idom-of dm))
                                    (set! (mut guard) (+ (load guard) 1))
                                    (continue))))))
                (load out))))))

(impl NodeOps IfNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (if (.never self) "Never" "If"))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; An If's type is a TUPLE, never constant, so the engine would not fold it anyway — but say so
  ;; explicitly rather than relying on that.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr IfNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; A Never is "never true", used to give an infinite loop an exit so that Stop stays reachable.
  ;; It always reports IF_BOTH and never idealizes.
  (compute [(self (ptr IfNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (if (.never self) (ty-if-both) (if-compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))
  (idealize [(self (ptr IfNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (if (.never self) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]) (if-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr IfNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (or (= (n-op other) OP-IF) (= (n-op other) OP-NEVER))
                (= (.never self) (.never (primitive/cast (ptr IfNode) (hdr other))))
                false))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> i64) (if (.never self) 131 127)))

(impl CFGOps IfNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; An If is a block TAIL. Its CProjs are the heads.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr IfNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr IfNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr IfNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn if--alloc [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps)) (pred (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (never bool) (opcode i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [IfNode] (alloc/create [IfNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.never p) never)
    (n-init! d opcode)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d pred)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; Build an If. NOT peepholed: the caller must build BOTH arms first — see `if-arms!`.
(defn if-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps)) (pred (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if--alloc ctrl (Some pred) false OP-IF))

;; "Never true", for giving an infinite loop an exit so that Stop stays reachable.
(defn never-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if--alloc ctrl (None [(dyn NodeOps)]) true OP-NEVER))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; PROJECTIONS — selecting one member out of a MULTI
;;
;; CProj selects a CONTROL member and is itself a control node and a block HEAD. Proj selects a
;; VALUE member and is a data node, PINNED to its multi's block.
;;
;; Both carry the member index and a debugging label, and both fold into the index — Simple hashes
;; and compares on `_idx` alone.
(defstruct CProjNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defstruct ProjNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))])

(defn cproj-idx [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (!= (n-op n) OP-CPROJ)
      (do (panic "cproj-idx: not a CProj") 0)
      (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr CProjNode) (hdr n)))))

(defn proj-idx [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (if (!= (n-op n) OP-PROJ)
      (do (panic "proj-idx: not a Proj") 0)
      (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ProjNode) (hdr n)))))

;; Read member `idx` out of the multi's tuple. When the multi is not (yet) a tuple, fall back to its
;; own extreme — `ty-oob`. High stays high because nothing is known yet; low goes to the bottom
;; because anything could be there.
(defn proj--compute [(d (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> i64)
  (match (n-in d 0)
    (None [] TY-TOP)
    (Some [m]
          (let [t (n-ty m)]
            (if (ty-tuple? t)
                (if (>= idx (ty-tuple-len t)) (ty-oob t) (ty-tuple-at t idx))
                (ty-oob t))))))

;; Simple's MultiNode.pcopy hook.  Most multis have no alternate source for a projection; call
;; multis live in a later module and contribute their implementation through the registered
;; extension at the bottom of this file, just as they do for `cfg-of`.
(defn multi-pcopy [(m (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (= (n-op m) OP-CALLEND) (pcopy-ext m idx) (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))

;; A CProj whose member is XCONTROL is DEAD and becomes the XCtrl node.
;;
;; And Simple's second rule: for an If whose OTHER arm is XCONTROL, this arm is the only survivor,
;; so it becomes the If's own incoming control — which is what actually removes the branch.
(defn cproj-idealize [(d (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (match (n-in d 0)
    (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
    (Some [m]
          (let [t (n-ty m)]
            (if (not (ty-tuple? t))
                (multi-pcopy m idx)
                (if (= (ty-tuple-at t idx) TY-XCTRL)
                    (Some (xctrl-make))
                    (if (and (or (= (n-op m) OP-IF) (= (n-op m) OP-NEVER))
                             (and (= (ty-tuple-len t) 2)
                                  (= (ty-tuple-at t (- 1 idx)) TY-XCTRL)))
                        (n-in m 0)
                        ;; Flip `CProj(If(Not(x)))`: rebuild the If over x with both projections
                        ;; constructed together, then select the opposite arm. The dependency is
                        ;; Simple's `addDep(iff.pred())`; if the predicate later becomes a Not, this
                        ;; projection must be retried.
                        (if (= (n-op m) OP-IF)
                            (match (n-in m 1)
                              (Some [pred]
                                    (do
                                      (n-add-dep! pred d)
                                      (if (= (n-op pred) OP-NOT)
                                          (let [arms (if-arms! (if-make (n-in! m 0) (n-in! pred 1)))]
                                            (Some (if (= idx 0)
                                                      (arms-false arms)
                                                      (arms-true arms))))
                                          (multi-pcopy m idx))))
                              (None [] (multi-pcopy m idx)))
                            (multi-pcopy m idx)))))))))

(impl NodeOps CProjNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr CProjNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (proj--compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (cproj-idealize (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr CProjNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-CPROJ)
                false
                (= (.idx self) (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr CProjNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> i64) (+ 137 (.idx self))))

(impl CFGOps CProjNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A CProj IS a block head.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr CProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr CProjNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(impl NodeOps ProjNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> (slice u8)) (.name self))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> bool) false)
  ;; Final Simple preserves a HIGH result projection from CallEnd while optimistic call-graph
  ;; linking is still discovering Returns. Replacing it with a detached TOP Constant loses the
  ;; edge that must sharpen later. Other Projs retain ordinary constant folding.
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
                  (-> bool)
                  (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
                    (match (n-in d 0)
                      (Some [m] (and (= (n-op m) OP-CALLEND) (ty-high? (n-ty d))))
                      (None [] false))))
  ;; PINNED: a Proj executes in its multi's block and code motion may not move it.
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ProjNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (proj--compute (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) (.idx self)))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ProjNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (match (n-in (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0)
              (Some [m] (multi-pcopy m (.idx self)))
              (None [] (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ProjNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))]
            (-> bool)
            (if (!= (n-op other) OP-PROJ)
                false
                (= (.idx self) (.idx (primitive/cast (ptr ProjNode) (hdr other))))))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ProjNode))] (-> i64) (+ 139 (.idx self))))

(defn cproj-make [(multi (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [CProjNode] (alloc/create [CProjNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) idx)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-CPROJ)
    (n-add-def! d (Some multi))
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

(defn proj-make [(multi (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64) (name (slice u8))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ProjNode] (alloc/create [ProjNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.idx p) idx)
    (set! (.name p) name)
    (n-init! d OP-PROJ)
    (n-add-def! d (Some multi))
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE MULTI WINDOW — the only sanctioned way to build an If's arms
;;
;; An If is IN PROGRESS until ALL of its projections exist. Peephole them one at a time and: the
;; first folds to ~ctrl, the If loses its last use, the kill cascade takes it, and the sibling then
;; rewrites against a corpse whose inputs were cleared but whose type was not — a stale buffer word.
;; Flat, that word happened to be pinned Start and the answer was accidentally right; one level of
;; nesting down it was a dead node.
;;
;; So there is deliberately NO way to make one arm alone. `if-arms!` builds both, then peepholes
;; both, and returns them as a pair.
(defstruct IfArms [(true-arm (dyn NodeOps)) (false-arm (dyn NodeOps))])

(defn if-arms! [(iff (dyn NodeOps))] (-> IfArms)
  (let [t (cproj-make iff IF-TRUE-IDX "True")
        f (cproj-make iff IF-FALSE-IDX "False")
        (mut r) (primitive/zeroed IfArms)]
    ;; BOTH exist before EITHER is peepholed. Keep each across the other's peephole, so a fold on
    ;; one cannot take the other down with it.
    (n-keep! t)
    (n-keep! f)
    (let [t2 (n-peephole! t)
          f2 (n-peephole! f)]
      (n-unkeep! t)
      (n-unkeep! f)
      (set! (.true-arm (mut r)) t2)
      (set! (.false-arm (mut r)) f2)
      (load r))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; RETURN
;;
;; Inputs are control, memory, value and RPC. The enclosing Fun is separate metadata, exactly as in
;; Simple: RPC is a graph edge cleared by inlining; ownership is not.
(const RETURN-CTRL 0)

(const RETURN-MEM 1)

(const RETURN-VALUE 2)

(const RETURN-RPC 3)

(defstruct ReturnNode [(hdr NodeHdr) (fun-nid i64)])

(impl NodeOps ReturnNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Return")
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ReturnNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (compute [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
           (-> i64)
           (let [d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self)]
             (ty-tuple-of3 (match (n-in d RETURN-CTRL) (Some [c] (n-ty c)) (None [] TY-XCTRL))
                           (match (n-in d RETURN-MEM) (Some [m] (n-ty m)) (None [] TY-TOP))
                           (match (n-in d RETURN-VALUE) (Some [v] (n-ty v)) (None [] TY-TOP)))))
  (idealize [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ReturnNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) (= (n-op other) OP-RETURN))
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> i64) 149))

(impl CFGOps ReturnNode
  (cfg-node [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> (dyn NodeOps)) (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))
  ;; A Return is a block TAIL.
  (block-head? [(self (ptr ReturnNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (cfg-idom [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
            (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
            (n-in-safe (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self) 0))
  (cfg-idepth [(self (ptr ReturnNode))]
              (-> i64)
              (cfg-idepth-default (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps self))))

(defn return-make [(ctrl (dyn NodeOps))
                   (mem (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
                   (val (dyn NodeOps))
                   (rpc (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
                   (fun (Option (dyn NodeOps)))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ReturnNode] (alloc/create [ReturnNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.fun-nid p) (match fun (Some [f] (n-nid f)) (None [] 0)))
    (n-init! d OP-RETURN)
    (n-add-def! d (Some ctrl))
    (n-add-def! d mem)
    (n-add-def! d (Some val))
    (n-add-def! d rpc)
    (invalidate-cfg!)
    (n-set-ty! d (compute p))
    d))

(defn return-fun-nid [(ret (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (.fun-nid (primitive/cast (ptr ReturnNode) (hdr ret))))

;; The second half of `cfg-of`, split only because one `if` chain of that depth is unreadable.
(defn cfg-of-more [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn CFGOps)))
  (let [op (n-op n)]
    (if (= op OP-LOOP)
        (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr LoopNode) (hdr n))))
        (if (or (= op OP-IF) (= op OP-NEVER))
            (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr IfNode) (hdr n))))
            (if (= op OP-CPROJ)
                (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr CProjNode) (hdr n))))
                (if (= op OP-RETURN)
                    (Some (primitive/make-dyn CFGOps (primitive/cast (ptr ReturnNode) (hdr n))))
                    (cfg-of-ext n)))))))

;; Accessors for the pair `if-arms!` returns.
(defn arms-true [(a IfArms)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (.true-arm a))

(defn arms-false [(a IfArms)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (.false-arm a))

;; Is this opcode a Phi, for the purposes of Region's arity invariant?
;;
;; A PARM IS A PHI — that is the whole design of `aot.node.call`, and it means a Parm must be counted
;; here. Miss it and dropping a call site from a Fun leaves every Parm one value too long, reading
;; the wrong caller's argument: a miscompile that typechecks cleanly.
(defn phi-like? [(op i64)] (-> bool)
  (or (= op OP-PHI) (= op OP-MEMPHI) (= op OP-PARM)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Extending `cfg-of` across a module boundary
;;
;; `cfg-of` must be the ONE place that knows which opcodes are control. But `Fun`, `Call` and
;; `CallEnd` are control and live in `aot.node.call`, which imports THIS module — so this module
;; cannot name them, and Coil has no cyclic imports.
;;
;; The answer is one registered extension, installed at boot. It is not a workaround: it keeps the
;; single-dispatch property (there is still exactly one `cfg-of`) while letting a later module
;; contribute the kinds it owns.
;;
;; An UNREGISTERED extension is a hard error the moment a call node is asked a control question,
;; rather than a quiet "not control" — which would make GCM try to place a Call as if it were data.
;; NOTE the `(ref …)`: a `defn` parameter of `dyn` type compiles to a reference, so the function-
;; pointer type has to say so or the two signatures do not match.
(defstruct CfgExt [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps))] (Option (dyn CFGOps)))) (installed bool)])

(defn cfg-ext [] (-> (ptr CfgExt))
  (primitive/alloc-static CfgExt))

(defn set-cfg-ext! [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps))] (Option (dyn CFGOps))))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.f (cfg-ext)) f) (set! (.installed (cfg-ext)) true) 0))

(defstruct PCopyExt
  [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps)) i64] (Option (dyn NodeOps)))) (installed bool)])

(defn pcopy-extension [] (-> (ptr PCopyExt))
  (primitive/alloc-static PCopyExt))

(defn set-pcopy-ext! [(f (fnptr c [(ref (dyn NodeOps)) i64] (Option (dyn NodeOps))))] (-> i64)
  (do (set! (.f (pcopy-extension)) f) (set! (.installed (pcopy-extension)) true) 0))

(defn pcopy-ext [(n (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (if (not (.installed (pcopy-extension)))
      (do
        (panic "multi-pcopy: CallEnd projection asked before ctrl-install-call-ext! ran")
        (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
      (primitive/call-ptr (.f (pcopy-extension)) n idx)))

;; Opcodes this module does not own but which ARE control. Asking about one before the extension is
;; installed is a wiring bug, and it says so.
(defn cfg--ext-owned? [(op i64)] (-> bool)
  (or (= op OP-FUN) (= op OP-CALL) (= op OP-CALLEND)))

(defn cfg-of-ext [(n (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (Option (dyn CFGOps)))
  (if (not (cfg--ext-owned? (n-op n)))
      (None [(dyn CFGOps)])
      (if (not (.installed (cfg-ext)))
          (do
            (panic-i "cfg-of: a control opcode owned by aot.node.call was asked a control question before ctrl-install-call-ext! ran (op)"
                     (n-op n))
            (None [(dyn CFGOps)]))
          (primitive/call-ptr (.f (cfg-ext)) n))))
